    @section XMLRPC XML-RPC

    XML-RPC is a lightweight but powerful XML over HTTP web service protocol.  The xml module includes builtin support
    for this protocol as described here.  You can find more information about XML-RPC, including specifications and
    examples at http://xmlrpc.org.

    Information about %Qore's XML-RPC serialization can be found below.

    <b>XML-RPC Serialization in %Qore</b>
    |!%Qore Type|!XML-RPC Type|!Description
    |\c string|\c string|direct conversion to UTF-8 string
    |\c integer|\c i4 or \c string|If the integer requires more than 32 bits to represent, then it is sent as a string
    |\c float|\c double|direct conversion
    |\c number|\c double|conversion to a double for serialization
    |\c boolean|\c boolean|direct conversion
    |\c date|\c iso8601|Absolute date/time values will convert to the default time zone for the calling context for \
        the output string if necessary. Note that relative date/time values (durations) will be serialized with the \
        same format as absolute date/time values.
    |\c binary|\c base64|direct conversion
    |\c list|\c array|direct conversion
    |\c hash|\c struct|direct conversion
    |all others|n/a|All other types will cause an \c XML-RPC-SERIALIZATION-ERROR to be raised.

    Strings preserve all XML character data, including whitespace-only values. Carriage returns are
    emitted as numeric references to survive XML line-ending normalization. Struct names can be empty
    or contain whitespace; an empty name is distinct from the literal name \c "!!empty-hash-key!!".
    Adjacent text and CDATA nodes are concatenated; comments and processing instructions contribute no text.
    An untyped value containing text defaults to string. Empty \c <value/> and \c <value></value> retain
    the historical %Qore \c NOTHING representation. Duplicate member names retain their last value.
    Characters forbidden by XML 1.0 raise \c XMLRPC-SERIALIZATION-ERROR; use \c binary for arbitrary bytes.

    @par Example
    @code
    hash<auto> customer = {"": "unnamed", "address": "First line\r\nSecond line"};
    string request = make_xmlrpc_call("customer.update", (customer,), 0, "UTF-16LE");
    hash<auto> parsed = parse_xmlrpc_call(request);
    @assert(parsed.params[0] == customer);
    @endcode

