    @section xml_constants XML Constants

    All constants (and classes and namespaces) provided by this module are created under the Qore namespace (the main
    namespace for the %Qore Programming Language).

    @section xml_codetags Function and Method Tags

    Function and method declarations use the language reference's @ref NOOP,
    @ref RUNTIME_NOOP, @ref RET_VALUE_ONLY, @ref CONSTANT and @ref DEPRECATED tags.

    @subsection xml_named_args NAMED_ARGS

    This flag indicates that callers can supply arguments by their documented parameter names.
    For example, \c make_xml(data: record) binds record to the data parameter.

    @section xmlreleasenotes Release Notes

    @subsection xml230 xml Module Version 2.3.0
    - WSDL SOAP/HTTP extensions validate binding ownership and cardinality;
      type-based document requests dispatch correctly with omitted actions
    - HTTP query and form requests validate every declared part even when empty;
      missing parts reject before callbacks while explicit empty values and zero-part operations work
    - WSDL target namespaces require absolute URIs when supplied; HTTP operation locations require relative URIs
    - XSD 1.0 anyURI rejects bare schemes such as <tt>a:</tt> and <tt>a:&#35;fragment</tt>;
      CMake detects affected system libraries
    - HTTP bindings accept empty operation locations, preserve Unicode URI boundaries, and route fixed query strings
    - MIME bindings match complete media types and declared parameters, support type/subtype wildcards,
      retain Content-Type parameters, and preserve opaque MIME bodies through HTTP clients and handlers
    - SOAP 1.2 action parameters are quoted and decoded; body-based routing uses the actual element name
    - HTTP URL replacement preserves literal parentheses and recognizes only declared message-part patterns;
      missing patterns fail before invocation, and repeated or failed descriptor updates preserve consistent state
    - HTTP and MIME leaf declarations validate grammar before binding; URI/token whitespace normalizes
      consistently for GET/POST methods, endpoint locations and MIME part references
    - SOAP binding declarations validate version-specific attributes and content before compilation;
      XML whitespace in transport URIs, fault names and header part references normalizes consistently
    - Core WSDL declarations validate child order, attributes, character content and required extensions
      before XML grouping; optional foreign extensions cannot shadow components or binding declarations
    - WSDL documentation and extension placement follow the corrected WSDL 1.1 schema; source and saved
      contracts retain extension payloads and their namespace scopes without treating them as SOAP data
    - Declared SOAP body fault details support explicit native decoding and retained literal XML,
      using the selected binding's fault schema while preserving ordinary client fault exceptions
    - Declared SOAP header faults can be serialized and decoded explicitly, including retained literal XML;
      SoapHandler callbacks can return header-processing errors without putting their data in Body detail
    - SOAP headers retain independent namespace/encoding descriptions; encoded header blocks carry their
      own encodingStyle, and malformed descriptions fail before invocation or restored use
    - Imported SOAP headers with identical local message names retain separate namespace-qualified value
      containers, including native and retained XML round trips, saved operations and SOAP consumers
    - Declared SOAP faults use independent binding descriptions and document-style detail; invalid concrete names,
      versions, use values and part counts reject before calls, including imported and saved services
    - Omitted SOAP body part lists select all message parts, including header-bound parts; explicit lists partition
      Body and Header. Overlapping native values retain independent ^body^ and ^headers^ maps for round trips
    - Literal SOAP serialization rejects missing selected body parts
    - Standard SOAP/HTTP bindings reject unsupported notification and solicit-response patterns before use,
      including manual registration and restored operation graphs; abstract operation discovery remains available
    - Explicit SOAP body selections preserve empty part lists and XML whitespace, reject invalid references,
      and constrain RPC decoding; normal response selections do not discard declared fault detail
    - WSDL overloads retain distinct input/output signatures and select concrete bindings by their declared labels;
      default labels follow abstract message order, and ambiguous selections reject
    - Empty RPC calls and responses retain their operation wrappers and decode as empty hashes;
      zero-part document messages retain an empty SOAP Body
    - WSDL SOAP decoding preserves body and header values when message/part names collide or body values are
      scalar; selected type and element wrappers retain their part keys within shared message containers
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5453">issue 5453</a>)
    - Requires Qore 3.0 with resolve_url() and HTTP effective-URL metadata. WSDL/XML Base and
      WebContentUtil share Qore URI resolution; native HTTP schemas use the final retrieval URL
      directly when resolving nested dependencies.
    - Private libxml2 IEEE parsing uses pinned fast_float for C++ libraries without floating-point
      std::from_chars, preserving nearest-even conversion and caller floating-point state on macOS.
    - Private libxml2 builds keep the upstream source root out of C++ header lookup, preventing
      VERSION from shadowing the standard &lt;version&gt; header on macOS case-insensitive filesystems.
    - WSDL serialization selects the SOAP version from the requested binding, including dual-version
      services, local namespace declarations and saved services; unused declarations do not select a protocol
    - Added XPF_PRESERVE_WHITESPACE for retaining all character whitespace inside elements with
      parse_xml(), schema parsing, XmlDoc and XmlReader, independently of xml:space default resets
    - WSDL anyURI and language scalar/provider conversions reject invalid lexical values while retaining
      URI spelling, language case and selected union identities through saved schemas and SOAP consumers
    - Added normalize_xsd_uri() for validated XSD anyURI whitespace normalization without resource
      retrieval, escaping or decoding; malformed XML characters and URI syntax reject with exact errors
    - WSDL validates schema annotation grammar and declaration IDs before grouping XML children,
      including saved schemas and SOAP contracts; mixed and empty documentation can be interleaved
    - WSDL applies canonical defaults to explicitly empty elements under their actual type;
      opt-in XsdDefaultValue carriers preserve this state through saved providers and SOAP consumers
    - Native empty-element defaults validate canonical spellings under the actual type and preserve
      declaration QName/NOTATION namespace identity; QName conversion and typed-value copies now
      propagate allocation failures with complete cleanup and recovery
    - Native XSD calendar values retain exact fractional seconds and extended years through parsing,
      copying, comparison and canonical declaration checks, with correct recoverable date timezones
    - WSDL checks canonical dateTime/time/date default/fixed declarations with exact fractional
      precision, calendar carries and recoverable date offsets, without replacing the selected value
    - The new canonical_xsd_float() API formats complete float/double lexical values at their
      selected precision; WSDL uses it to validate canonical default/fixed declarations while
      preserving the original selected value, lexical carrier and saved-provider behavior
    - Native float/double validation rejects malformed exponents, converts independently of locale
      and caller rounding state, and checks canonical default/fixed spellings without losing IEEE values
    - WSDL validates canonical binary default/fixed declarations while retaining the original
      binary octets and lexical carriers through saved schemas, providers and both SOAP bindings
    - Native binary default/fixed declarations validate uppercase hexadecimal and whitespace-free
      Base64 canonical spellings, including selected list/union members; Base64 rejects non-alphabet
      characters, and binary allocation failures propagate as internal errors with complete cleanup
    - WSDL applies the same canonical numeric/boolean declaration checks to elements and attributes,
      retaining the original selected values through saved schemas, providers and both SOAP bindings
    - Native integer, decimal and boolean default/fixed declarations validate their canonical spellings,
      including selected list/union members, attribute references and complex simple content;
      exact values and declaration namespace context are preserved
    - CMake detects missing canonical numeric constraint checks before selecting a libxml2 provider
    - WSDL schema values, SOAP messages and saved providers enforce selected ID/IDREF/IDREFS bindings
      within each assessed XML root, including forward references, list/union values and assessed wildcards
    - Native XSD validation rejects unresolved IDREF/IDREFS values and IDs identifying distinct elements;
      selected union/list values, defaults, forward references and subtree scope follow XSD 1.0
    - Failed identity union trials no longer change DOM ID tables; validation contexts release their
      ID binding registry on completion, rejection and allocation failure
    - CMake probes document ID bindings before accepting a libxml2 provider
    - Native anyType extensions use a consistent private particle layout and immutable builtin metadata,
      preventing invalid memory access during schema compilation
    - Empty group references retain their effective content when extending mixed types;
      CMake probes builtin particle traversal and the mixed-content derivation constraint
    - Native fixed constraints compare typed scalar and list values, including equivalent lexical forms
    - Native time comparisons normalize timezone offsets without inventing calendar dates
    - XSD 1.0 unsigned builtins reject signed lexical forms; nonNegativeInteger still accepts signed zero
    - Integer, list-token, string-value and URI allocation failures propagate as internal validation errors with cleanup;
      CMake probes these behaviors
      before selecting the system library or the private dependency
    - native schema validation treats empty CDATA as zero characters, preserves default/fixed assessment,
      and permits XML whitespace CDATA in element-only content; actual content on nilled elements still rejects
    - CMake detects native character-content behavior before selecting system libxml2; the private correction
      preserves text-buffer ownership and original XML data
    - strict element wildcards assess an available xsi:type before requiring a global element declaration
    - schema declarations accept whitespace-only CDATA; annotation text and markup are preserved during compilation
    - DOM and streaming validation report wildcard ID conflicts, including absent declared ID uses
      and ID restrictions; CMake tests the installed library before selecting a provider
    - Native ID schema constraints follow base-type ancestry, and referenced attribute constraints
      are validated before computing their typed default/fixed values
    - Native schema construction rejects inconsistent explicit and implicit element declarations,
      including unused groups, imported members, inherited models and anonymous nested types;
      CMake detects missing checks before selecting the installed libxml2
    - Anonymous simple types inherit schema finalDefault exclusions during DOM and stream schema
      construction; CMake detects dependencies that omit these controls and selects the corrected provider
    - Finite XSD occurrence bounds retain exact values above native integer ranges during schema
      construction and DOM/reader execution, including nullable groups and inherited particles
    - Native counter rollback and diagnostics preserve exact values; unbounded repetitions cannot
      overflow their counters; CMake detects incomplete occurrence-range backports
    - XSD schema construction checks exact counted particle attribution before automaton reduction,
      including unused groups and present groups inside an empty surrounding language
    - Fixed repeated-element boundaries select the correct declaration; abstract substitution heads
      cannot take a matching name from a usable local declaration
    - Nested finite repetitions retain every counter increment, and nullable groups avoid counting
      repeated empty iterations; CMake tests these behaviors before choosing a system libxml2
    - XSD automaton compilation preserves distinct particle positions for local/global elements,
      shared group uses, all members and wildcards, rejecting ambiguous declarations before validation
    - CMake probes particle identity before selecting a system libxml2 backport
    - XSD occurrence attributes accept signed nonnegative integers and XML whitespace around
      unbounded; local elements with minOccurs=maxOccurs=0 contribute no content-model particle
    - CMake probes occurrence parsing and zero-count validation before selecting the installed
      libxml2, and applies checked corrections to the private fallback when needed
    - XML-RPC strings and struct names retain whitespace, carriage returns, empty names and adjacent
      text/CDATA chunks; comments and processing instructions do not split a scalar value
    - XML-RPC generation supports UTF-16 output and rejects characters forbidden by XML 1.0;
      signed 32-bit minimum values keep the integer wire type and legacy encoding wrappers retain arguments
    - XML-RPC empty values preserve following collection members, duplicate keys release replaced values,
      and malformed requests produce the expected fault when no optional logger is configured
    - native XSD validation accepts correctly declared unparsed ENTITY/ENTITIES values in elements,
      attributes and simple content; computed values support enumeration, list and union constraints
    - ordered unions select a datatype before checking ENTITY declarations; failed document constraints
      cannot redirect a selected ENTITY value to a later string member
    - built-in ENTITIES, IDREFS and NMTOKENS enforce their minimum of one item, including derived types;
      CMake probes the installed library and applies checked native corrections to the private fallback
    - XSD schema construction rejects contradictory minimum and maximum lengths, including inherited bounds
    - XML conversion releases partially built values when schema callbacks report an error at a node
      or end-of-document boundary, including rejected defaults on empty elements
    - XSD include/import/redefine locations accept raw Unicode and spaces while preserving URI
      percent escapes, path separators and XML Base values; CMake checks this behavior before
      choosing an installed libxml2 and applies checked fixes to the private fallback
    - runtime schema hints preserve complete anyURI values and streaming document bases;
      namespace and no-namespace hints can both contribute schemas on one element
    - HTTP schema loads encode raw URI characters once; optional schema diagnostics use detailed
      debug logging without writing to application stdout during ordinary Debug execution
    - XmlReader::schemaValidateString() attaches XSD text before reading; schemaValidate() retains
      its file/URI behavior with corrected documentation. Invalid replacement schemas preserve the
      prior validator, and native schema ownership remains valid through reader cleanup
    - XSD resource and catalog loads enforce filesystem/network policy; HTTP redirects preserve
      import base URIs, and independent callback streams support nested compilation and cancellation
    - XmlReader cursor conversion returns scalar text and empty values safely, stops at the current
      element boundary, and preserves following siblings; discarded results still advance the reader
    - XSD unions can accept a later member after a QName candidate rejects an unbound prefix;
      CMake verifies ordered matching and the bundled libxml2 isolates trial diagnostics while preserving
      errors for invalid QName values, failed unions and enumeration mismatches
    - CMake verifies QName namespace identity in both DOM and streaming XSD validation; the private libxml2
      fallback resolves the implicit \c xml prefix and equates absent and explicitly empty QName namespaces
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::convert_xsd_float "convert_xsd_float()" for strict XML Schema floating-point
      conversion with direct binary32/binary64 nearest rounding, signed zero, subnormals and special values;
      native integers and arbitrary-precision numbers retain their source value until final IEEE rounding
    - XML text and CDATA generation preserve carriage returns through line-ending normalization;
      formatting does not add character data around CDATA or inside preserved-space content
    - XML data parsing retains whitespace-only scalar values and ordered mixed text, honors inherited
      \c xml:space, and preserves repeated-child grouping when omitting element-only indentation
    - CMake checks actual libxml2 namespace-URI decoding and uses a checksum-pinned private
      libxml2 2.15.4 build through FetchContent when the installed library is missing or fails;
      fixed distribution backports remain supported, with explicit system/bundled and offline
      source options documented in README (CMake 3.18 or later is required)
    - XML attribute generation uses character references for tabs, line feeds and carriage returns,
      preserving their values through XML attribute normalization and retained subtree extraction
    - added validated XML element fragments through \c ^xml^ keys in XML generation, preserving lexical
      content, namespaces and node order; generation supports cooperative cancellation and retains its
      original exception on failure
    - fixed XML string readers decoding already-converted UTF-8 bytes again according to an old XML
      declaration; string parsing now honors the Qore encoding and rejects embedded-NUL truncation
    - fixed UTF-16 XML document and fragment output by encoding the complete generated markup
    - schema import callback exceptions no longer produce a misleading secondary skipped-import warning
    - native XSD annotations accept foreign namespace attributes regardless of local name and
      validate documentation source URIs without dereferencing them; configure probes both behaviors
    - native XSD selectors and fields reject trailing XPath union separators; the pattern compiler
      rejects empty input with correct cleanup, and configure detects affected system libraries
    - native schema component QName references collapse surrounding XML whitespace before lookup;
      dictionary and implicit xml namespace allocation failures preserve memory-error diagnostics
    - native key fields reject nillable element declarations even for non-nil values, while
      attribute fields on nillable owners remain valid; configure detects affected system libraries
    - native identity fields now assess the four built-in xsi attributes, distinguish list and atomic values
      through union/default conversion, and preserve empty lists independently of nil; identity formatting
      uses owned buffers, retains QName/NOTATION expanded names, and reports allocation failures;
      invalid attribute matches release their XPath state; configure-time probes detect affected libraries
    - native unique/keyref constraints treat nil as a missing identity value under the approved
      interpretation, preserving selected-node cardinality and the key nillable-declaration restriction;
      allocation failures in field capture retain their validation context and memory-error category
    - native key/unique tables retain distinct keys after sibling conflicts, prefer locally selected
      entries, and discard child conflict markers before ancestor merging; allocation failures retain
      ownership and memory-error diagnostics, and CMake detects affected system libraries
    - native XSD NOTATION declarations validate identifiers, normalized names and allowed attributes;
      used NOTATION types require an inherited enumeration, with checked schema allocation recovery
    - the bundled libxml2 build frees temporary catalog errors before restoring the prior error record
    - enabled CMake policies through 3.31 for clean configuration with CMake 4
    - CMake selects C++17 for Qore 3.0 public headers and retains C++11 for older Qore releases
    - <a href="../../WSDL/html/index.html">WSDL</a> module changes:
      - added \c XsdXmlValue for explicitly retaining XML lexical values, namespace contexts and complete
        ordered content, with reader/data views, validated Serializable reconstruction and XML generation
      - fixed serializing \c xs:unsignedShort and \c xs:unsignedInt values above the corresponding signed limits
      - fixed serializing native floating-point infinities and NaN with the lexical forms required by XSD
      - added offline W3C Databinding interoperability regression tests and an optional corpus survey
      - pinned the complete W3C corpus and an offline import catalog with checksum verification and
        safe extraction; fixed cross-schema contamination of survey resolver diagnostics
      - added independent offline Xerces-J source adjudication against inline WSDL schemas, normative
        checks for validator disagreements, and pinned CXF binding/attachment contracts for further testing
      - added an adjudicated strict corpus selection with independent request/response runs, explicit
        binding identity, exact-value checks, complete stage accounting, and worker cancellation regressions
      - completed whole-archive source accounting, aggregate namespace/import defect adjudication, and
        historical toolkit schema/parse diagnostics with explicit implementation-phase ownership
      - added support for the XSD \c include statement
      - fixed resolving relative \c schemaLocation values; they are now resolved against the location of the document
        containing the reference and not against the location of the top-level WSDL
      - fixed handling element \c ref attributes with no namespace prefix
      - fixed handling element declarations with no type information; such elements now have type \c xs:anyType
      - fixed handling \c simpleType \c union definitions with more than one anonymous member type definition
      - fixed resolving \c simpleType \c restriction base types declared after the restricting type in the schema
      - fixed serializing XSD attribute values; attribute values are now serialized according to their type like
        element values, so dates, booleans, and binary values are written in their XSD representation
      - fixed serializing \c xs:boolean values given as strings; \c "false" and \c "0" are now serialized as
        \c "false" instead of \c "true"
      - fixed handling \c complexType \c choice definitions declared with \c minOccurs="0"; such choices no longer
        require a member to be present
      - fixed a runtime error serializing a \c complexType \c choice with no member supplied; a descriptive
        exception is now thrown if the choice is required
      - fixed a runtime error retrieving the data provider type for a \c complexType \c choice definition
      - fixed deserializing messages whose parts are split between the SOAP header and the SOAP body; the body parts
        are now taken from the \c parts attribute of the \c soap:body element, or, if that attribute is not present,
        from the message parts not bound to a SOAP header
      - fixed reporting enumeration values for numeric, boolean, and date types; they were always reported as strings,
        which also caused an error when retrieving the data provider type for such a type
      - data provider types for scalar XSD types are now soft types (ex: \c softint), matching the values accepted
        when serializing
      - fixed the data provider type for \c xs:boolean; string values are now parsed according to the XSD boolean
        lexical space, so that \c "false" and \c "0" are converted to @ref False "False"
      - fixed generating example messages for \c complexType \c choice definitions and \c simpleType \c union
        definitions
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5405">issue 5405</a>)
      - fixed handling nested XSD model groups; elements declared in an \c xs:sequence, \c xs:choice, or \c xs:all
        nested in another model group were silently dropped from the type, so they could neither be serialized nor
        deserialized
      - added support for \c xs:choice alternatives declared as nested model groups; all the elements of a single
        alternative can now be serialized and deserialized together
      - fixed the data provider type for a \c complexType with a \c sequence containing a \c choice block; the
        members of the choice were not reported as fields
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5410">issue 5410</a>)
      - fixed combining multiple \c xs:pattern facets declared in the same restriction; they were intersected, so
        a type declaring one pattern per alternative form (ex: one per IPv6 textual form) rejected every value.
        XSD 1.0 part 2 4.3.4.3 combines sibling patterns with \c "|", so a value matching any one of them is
        valid; patterns declared in different derivation steps continue to be intersected
      - fixed anchoring \c xs:pattern facets with a top-level alternation; the pattern is now wrapped in a
        non-capturing group, so that \c "a|b" is anchored as \c "^(?:a|b)$" and no longer accepts any value
        merely starting with \c "a" or ending with \c "b"
      - XSD regular expressions are now translated to PCRE before use; the two languages are not the same:
        \c "^" and \c "$" are ordinary characters in XSD, XSD has the \c "\i", \c "\I", \c "\c", and
        \c "\C" escapes and character class subtraction that PCRE does not have, and \c "\s", \c "\w", and
        \c "\d" denote different character sets in the two languages
      - fixed counting digits for the \c totalDigits and \c fractionDigits facets; both facets constrain the
        digits of the value, so insignificant zeros do not count
      - example messages generated by \c WSMessageHelper now satisfy the constraining facets declared for their
        types; previously the placeholder values used for the base types normally violated them, so a generated
        example message could not be serialized
      - added support for the XSD \c "\p{IsXxx}" Unicode block escapes; PCRE has no block support, so such a
        pattern previously failed to compile with a \c REGEX-COMPILATION-ERROR
      - added \c whiteSpace processing; \c whiteSpace is a normalization facet rather than a constraint, so the
        lexical value is now normalized before it is validated.  \c xs:string preserves whitespace,
        \c xs:normalizedString replaces every tab, newline, and carriage return with a space, and \c xs:token
        and every other built-in type additionally collapse runs of spaces and trim the value
        (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5411">issue 5411</a>)
    - new <a href="../../CdaDataProvider/html/index.html">CdaDataProvider</a> user module providing
      CDA / HL7 v3 XML document parsing, XSD validation with import/include resolution, and
      metadata, participant, section, and structured entry extraction through the DataProvider API
    - new <a href="../../XmlRpcClientIo/html/index.html">XmlRpcClientIo</a> user module providing a
      pure-Qore XML-RPC client with async I/O via \c HttpClientIo with automatic protocol negotiation
      (HTTP/3 &rarr; HTTP/2 &rarr; HTTP/1.1) and connection pooling; this is the recommended replacement
      for the native \c XmlRpcClient class
    - marked the native \c XmlRpcClient class as deprecated in favor of \c XmlRpcClientIo
    - added @ref Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile "Qore::Xml::XmlDoc::validateSchemaFile()" for validating against XSDs that span
      multiple files; libxml2 resolves \c xsd:import and \c xsd:include \c schemaLocation entries
      relative to the file path, which is required for industry message standards (UN/CEFACT,
      CargoXML, etc.) delivered as a tree of XSDs

    @subsection xml220 xml Module Version 2.2.0
    - new @ref webcontentutilintro "WebContentUtil" user module providing reusable web
      content processing primitives consolidated from \c qctl, \c saasctl, and the planned
      \c QorusAiWebCrawler service:
      - @ref WebContentUtil::WebCrawler "WebCrawler" — parallel BFS web crawler built on
        @ref httpclientiointro "HttpClientIo" with HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 multiplexing,
        atomic visited-index dedup, automatic 429/503 throttling, configurable User-Agent
        and max response size, and full SSRF protection
      - @ref WebContentUtil::SsrfFilter "SsrfFilter" — server-side request forgery
        protection rejecting private IPv4 (RFC 1918, loopback, link-local, CGNAT, multicast),
        private IPv6 (ULA, link-local, IPv4-mapped), Kubernetes service DNS, \c .local,
        and cloud metadata endpoints by default
      - @ref WebContentUtil::RobotsTxt "RobotsTxt" — RFC 9309 robots.txt parser with
        \c User-agent group matching, \c Allow / \c Disallow precedence, \c Crawl-delay,
        and \c Sitemap extraction
      - @ref WebContentUtil::UrlNormalizer "UrlNormalizer" — full RFC 3986 URL normalization
        (fragment stripping, path resolution, lowercase scheme/host, default port stripping,
        trailing slash policy, percent-encoding canonicalization, query parameter sorting)
      - @ref WebContentUtil::HtmlExtractor "HtmlExtractor" — HTML to plain text extraction
        with element stripping
      - @ref WebContentUtil::TextChunker "TextChunker" — paragraph/sentence chunking with
        configurable target token size and overlap, suitable for embedding pipelines
      - @ref WebContentUtil::LocalDocReader "LocalDocReader" — recursive local HTML
        directory reader with file type filtering and directory exclusion

